1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,930
Scripts get run again.

2
00:00:01,930 --> 00:00:03,491
A scheduler fires twice.

3
00:00:03,491 --> 00:00:04,986
A deploy fails halfway,

4
00:00:04,986 --> 00:00:06,677
and someone runs it again.

5
00:00:06,677 --> 00:00:09,342
If your script only works the first time,

6
00:00:09,342 --> 00:00:11,423
the second run can break things.

7
00:00:11,973 --> 00:00:13,744
Today you learn two skills.

8
00:00:13,744 --> 00:00:14,991
Idempotent scripts,

9
00:00:14,991 --> 00:00:16,762
which give the same result,

10
00:00:16,762 --> 00:00:18,600
however many times they run.

11
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:19,584
And ShellCheck,

12
00:00:19,584 --> 00:00:23,258
a free tool that finds mistakes before you run anything.

13
00:00:23,808 --> 00:00:26,603
Every demo is a real recording on Ubuntu.

14
00:00:28,653 --> 00:00:29,066
First,

15
00:00:29,066 --> 00:00:31,890
what goes wrong when a script runs twice.

16
00:00:31,890 --> 00:00:33,474
Here is a setup script.

17
00:00:33,474 --> 00:00:34,989
Type: cat bad dot S H.

18
00:00:35,539 --> 00:00:41,491
It makes a folder, and adds a setting to a file. Run it once. Type: bash bad dot S H.

19
00:00:42,041 --> 00:00:47,268
Silent, so it worked. Now run it a second time. Type: bash bad dot S H.

20
00:00:47,818 --> 00:00:48,396
An error.

21
00:00:48,396 --> 00:00:50,066
The folder already exists.

22
00:00:50,066 --> 00:00:52,186
And what about the settings file?

23
00:00:52,186 --> 00:00:53,663
Type: cat app dot conf.

24
00:00:54,213 --> 00:00:55,764
The line is there twice.

25
00:00:55,764 --> 00:00:56,862
Run it ten times,

26
00:00:56,862 --> 00:00:58,283
and you get ten lines.

27
00:00:58,283 --> 00:01:01,643
A script like this makes a mess when it is repeated,

28
00:01:01,643 --> 00:01:03,258
or retried after a crash.

29
00:01:05,308 --> 00:01:08,679
Now the better version. Type: cat good dot S H.

30
00:01:09,229 --> 00:01:10,206
Three changes.

31
00:01:10,206 --> 00:01:14,463
M K dir with dash P creates the folder only if it is missing.

32
00:01:14,463 --> 00:01:18,930
The grep check adds the setting only if it is not there already.

33
00:01:18,930 --> 00:01:23,257
And the strict mode line stops the script on any real failure.

34
00:01:23,257 --> 00:01:23,746
Run it.

35
00:01:23,746 --> 00:01:25,421
Type: bash good dot S H.

36
00:01:25,971 --> 00:01:30,301
Run it again, with the same command. Type: bash good dot S H.

37
00:01:30,851 --> 00:01:36,910
No error, and no output. Nothing needed doing. Check the file. Type: cat app dot conf.

38
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:38,049
One line,

39
00:01:38,049 --> 00:01:40,011
however many times you run it.

40
00:01:40,011 --> 00:01:41,254
That is idempotent.

41
00:01:41,254 --> 00:01:43,412
Running it again changes nothing.

42
00:01:45,462 --> 00:01:49,129
Bash gives you repeat-safe switches built in.

43
00:01:49,129 --> 00:01:49,618
First,

44
00:01:49,618 --> 00:01:51,247
M K dir with dash P,

45
00:01:51,247 --> 00:01:52,062
run twice,

46
00:01:52,062 --> 00:01:54,099
then print the exit code.

47
00:01:54,099 --> 00:01:56,055
Type: mkdir dash P logs,

48
00:01:56,055 --> 00:01:56,870
semicolon,

49
00:01:56,870 --> 00:01:58,337
mkdir dash P logs,

50
00:01:58,337 --> 00:01:59,151
semicolon,

51
00:01:59,151 --> 00:02:01,270
echo dollar question mark.

52
00:02:01,820 --> 00:02:02,212
Zero.

53
00:02:02,212 --> 00:02:04,330
No error on the second run.

54
00:02:04,330 --> 00:02:04,722
Next,

55
00:02:04,722 --> 00:02:05,977
R M with dash F,

56
00:02:05,977 --> 00:02:09,820
which does not complain when the file is missing.

57
00:02:09,820 --> 00:02:11,153
Type: R M dash F,

58
00:02:11,153 --> 00:02:12,565
nothing dot T X T,

59
00:02:12,565 --> 00:02:13,349
semicolon,

60
00:02:13,349 --> 00:02:15,388
echo dollar question mark.

61
00:02:15,938 --> 00:02:16,839
Zero again.

62
00:02:16,839 --> 00:02:17,249
Last,

63
00:02:17,249 --> 00:02:18,887
L N with dash S F N,

64
00:02:18,887 --> 00:02:21,754
which replaces a link if it exists,

65
00:02:21,754 --> 00:02:23,556
and creates it if not.

66
00:02:23,556 --> 00:02:24,621
Run it twice,

67
00:02:24,621 --> 00:02:25,440
then look.

68
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:27,160
Type: L N dash S F N,

69
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:27,569
logs,

70
00:02:27,569 --> 00:02:27,897
cur,

71
00:02:27,897 --> 00:02:28,716
semicolon,

72
00:02:28,716 --> 00:02:29,208
again,

73
00:02:29,208 --> 00:02:30,027
semicolon,

74
00:02:30,027 --> 00:02:30,928
L S dash L,

75
00:02:30,928 --> 00:02:31,255
cur.

76
00:02:31,805 --> 00:02:36,264
One link, and no error. Prefer these forms, so a second run is harmless.

77
00:02:38,314 --> 00:02:38,661
Next,

78
00:02:38,661 --> 00:02:39,426
ShellCheck,

79
00:02:39,426 --> 00:02:44,429
a free tool that reads your script and finds mistakes before you run it.

80
00:02:44,429 --> 00:02:46,444
Here is a script with a typo.

81
00:02:46,444 --> 00:02:48,042
Type: cat typo dot S H.

82
00:02:48,592 --> 00:02:50,599
The variable is called name,

83
00:02:50,599 --> 00:02:52,820
but the echo line says N M A E.

84
00:02:52,820 --> 00:02:55,185
Bash would quietly print nothing.

85
00:02:55,185 --> 00:02:56,618
Let ShellCheck look.

86
00:02:56,618 --> 00:02:58,768
Type: shellcheck typo dot S H.

87
00:02:59,318 --> 00:03:01,047
ShellCheck shows the line,

88
00:03:01,047 --> 00:03:02,576
the problem and a code,

89
00:03:02,576 --> 00:03:04,305
S C twenty one fifty four.

90
00:03:04,305 --> 00:03:06,101
The variable is referenced,

91
00:03:06,101 --> 00:03:07,231
but not assigned.

92
00:03:07,231 --> 00:03:08,561
Check its exit code.

93
00:03:08,561 --> 00:03:09,292
Type: echo,

94
00:03:09,292 --> 00:03:10,689
dollar question mark.

95
00:03:11,239 --> 00:03:11,522
One.

96
00:03:11,522 --> 00:03:12,655
A non-zero exit,

97
00:03:12,655 --> 00:03:17,186
so a pipeline can fail the build when ShellCheck finds problems.

98
00:03:17,186 --> 00:03:17,894
On Ubuntu,

99
00:03:17,894 --> 00:03:21,009
install it with sudo apt install shellcheck.

100
00:03:23,059 --> 00:03:26,102
ShellCheck can often fix the problem for you.

101
00:03:26,102 --> 00:03:29,820
Here is a script that breaks on file names with spaces.

102
00:03:29,820 --> 00:03:31,443
Type: cat buggy dot S H.

103
00:03:31,993 --> 00:03:33,127
Without quotes,

104
00:03:33,127 --> 00:03:37,209
the file name my file dot T X T splits into two words.

105
00:03:37,209 --> 00:03:39,174
Ask ShellCheck for a diff.

106
00:03:39,174 --> 00:03:40,460
Type: shellcheck,

107
00:03:40,460 --> 00:03:40,989
dash F,

108
00:03:40,989 --> 00:03:41,367
diff,

109
00:03:41,367 --> 00:03:42,425
buggy dot S H.

110
00:03:42,975 --> 00:03:45,738
Lines starting with a minus are removed,

111
00:03:45,738 --> 00:03:48,569
and lines starting with a plus are added.

112
00:03:48,569 --> 00:03:51,194
The fix is quotes around the variable.

113
00:03:51,194 --> 00:03:53,404
Apply it with the patch command.

114
00:03:53,404 --> 00:03:56,374
Type: shellcheck dash F diff buggy dot S H,

115
00:03:56,374 --> 00:03:56,719
pipe,

116
00:03:56,719 --> 00:03:58,100
patch buggy dot S H.

117
00:03:58,650 --> 00:04:02,704
Patched. Look at the script again. Type: cat buggy dot S H.

118
00:04:03,254 --> 00:04:04,163
Quotes added.

119
00:04:04,163 --> 00:04:05,912
Now run ShellCheck again,

120
00:04:05,912 --> 00:04:08,780
and print clean only if it finds nothing.

121
00:04:08,780 --> 00:04:10,949
Type: shellcheck buggy dot S H,

122
00:04:10,949 --> 00:04:11,228
and,

123
00:04:11,228 --> 00:04:11,508
and,

124
00:04:11,508 --> 00:04:12,278
echo clean.

125
00:04:12,828 --> 00:04:18,118
Clean. Always read a patch before you trust it. But this is a fast way to learn the fixes.

126
00:04:20,168 --> 00:04:26,120
Now one script that is idempotent, and clean under ShellCheck. Type: cat site dot S H.

127
00:04:26,670 --> 00:04:28,032
It ensures a folder,

128
00:04:28,032 --> 00:04:29,463
a setting and a link,

129
00:04:29,463 --> 00:04:30,757
all safe to repeat.

130
00:04:30,757 --> 00:04:32,868
Check it with ShellCheck first.

131
00:04:32,868 --> 00:04:34,912
Type: shellcheck site dot S H,

132
00:04:34,912 --> 00:04:35,184
and,

133
00:04:35,184 --> 00:04:35,457
and,

134
00:04:35,457 --> 00:04:36,206
echo clean.

135
00:04:36,756 --> 00:04:39,977
Clean. Now run it. Type: bash site dot S H.

136
00:04:40,527 --> 00:04:42,280
It ensured everything.

137
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:43,316
Run it again,

138
00:04:43,316 --> 00:04:45,866
and count the lines in the file.

139
00:04:45,866 --> 00:04:47,779
Type: bash site dot S H,

140
00:04:47,779 --> 00:04:48,576
semicolon,

141
00:04:48,576 --> 00:04:48,894
W C,

142
00:04:48,894 --> 00:04:49,452
dash L,

143
00:04:49,452 --> 00:04:51,365
conf slash app dot conf.

144
00:04:51,915 --> 00:04:56,352
Still one line. Safe to run, safe to retry, and safe to schedule.

145
00:04:58,402 --> 00:04:59,725
Four myths to clear up.

146
00:05:00,275 --> 00:05:02,526
Myth one: if a script worked once,

147
00:05:02,526 --> 00:05:03,255
it is fine.

148
00:05:03,255 --> 00:05:03,453
No.

149
00:05:03,453 --> 00:05:04,314
Run it twice,

150
00:05:04,314 --> 00:05:05,374
and it may fail,

151
00:05:05,374 --> 00:05:06,632
or repeat its work.

152
00:05:07,182 --> 00:05:09,421
Myth two: ShellCheck finds every bug.

153
00:05:09,421 --> 00:05:09,603
No.

154
00:05:09,603 --> 00:05:11,418
It finds many common mistakes,

155
00:05:11,418 --> 00:05:13,475
but it cannot know what you meant.

156
00:05:14,025 --> 00:05:17,647
Myth three: a clean ShellCheck means safe for production.

157
00:05:17,647 --> 00:05:17,838
No.

158
00:05:17,838 --> 00:05:19,871
Test the script on a copy first.

159
00:05:20,421 --> 00:05:23,840
Myth four: idempotent means the script does nothing.

160
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:24,038
No.

161
00:05:24,038 --> 00:05:26,142
It does the work the first time,

162
00:05:26,142 --> 00:05:28,378
and then finds nothing left to do.

163
00:05:28,928 --> 00:05:31,424
Same result. Check first. Lint first.

164
00:05:33,474 --> 00:05:38,039
Here is how you check your own understanding. Six points, and you need all six.

165
00:05:38,589 --> 00:05:44,157
Explain idempotent in one sentence: the same result, however many times it runs.

166
00:05:44,707 --> 00:05:51,065
Use M K dir dash P, R M dash F and L N dash S F N, so repeats are harmless.

167
00:05:51,615 --> 00:05:55,263
Check before you change a file, so a setting is never added twice.

168
00:05:55,813 --> 00:05:59,717
Run ShellCheck, and read the code and the line number of each finding.

169
00:06:00,267 --> 00:06:05,344
Fix the findings, with quotes or the diff output, and run ShellCheck until it is clean.

170
00:06:05,894 --> 00:06:10,118
Prove it: run the script twice, and show that the second run changes nothing.

171
00:06:10,668 --> 00:06:12,289
To pass, you need all six.

172
00:06:14,339 --> 00:06:15,043
Your turn,

173
00:06:15,043 --> 00:06:17,505
in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

174
00:06:17,505 --> 00:06:19,264
Install ShellCheck first,

175
00:06:19,264 --> 00:06:21,585
with sudo apt install shellcheck.

176
00:06:21,585 --> 00:06:23,555
Type every command yourself.

177
00:06:24,105 --> 00:06:27,265
One: write a script that makes a folder and appends a line,

178
00:06:27,265 --> 00:06:29,140
and run it twice to watch it break.

179
00:06:29,690 --> 00:06:35,215
Two: make it repeat-safe with M K dir dash P and a grep check, and run it three times.

180
00:06:35,765 --> 00:06:37,911
Three: try M K dir dash P,

181
00:06:37,911 --> 00:06:41,294
R M dash F and L N dash S F N twice each,

182
00:06:41,294 --> 00:06:43,274
and read the exit codes.

183
00:06:43,824 --> 00:06:48,368
Four: write a script with a typo in a variable name, and let ShellCheck find it.

184
00:06:48,918 --> 00:06:51,686
Five: write a script with an unquoted variable,

185
00:06:51,686 --> 00:06:54,572
and fix it with shellcheck dash F diff and patch.

186
00:06:55,122 --> 00:07:00,071
Six: run ShellCheck on a script from earlier in this course, and fix every finding.

187
00:07:00,621 --> 00:07:03,928
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

188
00:07:19,478 --> 00:07:20,353
Quick check.

189
00:07:20,903 --> 00:07:22,631
What does idempotent mean?

190
00:07:23,181 --> 00:07:25,698
Which flag makes M K dir safe to repeat?

191
00:07:26,248 --> 00:07:29,576
What exit code does ShellCheck give, when it finds problems?

192
00:07:30,126 --> 00:07:32,579
How do you prove a script is idempotent?

193
00:07:33,129 --> 00:07:38,847
Objective covered: MC05-O08.

194
00:07:39,397 --> 00:07:41,466
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

195
00:07:52,016 --> 00:07:55,152
Next lesson: a deploy, health check and rollback script.

196
00:07:55,702 --> 00:07:59,030
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
